.TH "TOMO" "1" "June 11, 2024" ""
.SH NAME
tomo \- The programming language of tomorrow.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.TP
Run a program:
\f[B]tomo\f[R] \f[I]program.tm\f[R] [[\f[B]\-\-\f[R]]
\f[I]args\&...\f[R]]
.TP
Transpile tomo files to C files:
\f[B]tomo\f[R] \f[B]\-t\f[R] \f[I]file1.tm\f[R] \f[I]file2.tm\f[R]\&...
.TP
Compile files to static object files:
\f[B]tomo\f[R] \f[B]\-c\f[R] \f[I]file1.tm\f[R] \f[I]file2.tm\f[R]\&...
.TP
Compile files to a executables:
\f[B]tomo\f[R] \f[B]\-e\f[R] \f[I]file1.tm\f[R] \f[I]file2.tm\f[R]\&...
.TP
Build a package:
\f[B]tomo\f[R] \f[B]\-p\f[R] \f[I]file1.tm\f[R] \f[I]file2.tm\f[R]\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Tomo is a programming language that is statically typed, compiled,
small, and garbage\-collected, with concise syntax and built\-in support
for high\-performance, low\-overhead datastructures.
It compiles by first outputting C code, which is then compiled using the
Zig toolchain bundled with the Tomo installation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-\-changelog\f[R]
Print the compiler change log and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-transpile\f[R], \f[B]\-t\f[R] \f[I]file1.tm\f[R] \f[I]file2.tm\f[R]\&...
Transpile the given files to C.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-compile\-exe\f[R], \f[B]\-e\f[R] \f[I]file1.tm\f[R] \f[I]file2.tm\f[R]\&...
Compile the given files to executables.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-compile\-obj\f[R], \f[B]\-c\f[R] \f[I]file1.tm\f[R] \f[I]file2.tm\f[R]\&...
Compile the given files to static objects.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-help\f[R], \f[B]\-h\f[R]
Print the usage and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-package\f[R], \f[B]\-p\f[R] \f[I]folder1\f[R] \f[I]folder2\f[R]\&...
Compile the input folders to shared packages.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-install\f[R], \f[B]\-I\f[R]
When using \f[B]\-e\f[R] or \f[B]\-p\f[R], install the resulting
executables or packages.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-build\-info\f[R], \f[B]\-b\f[R] \f[I]file1\f[R] \f[I]file2\f[R]\&...
Print the build info embedded in the given compiled files (executables
or package archives).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-extract\-source\f[R], \f[B]\-x\f[R] \f[I]program1\f[R] \f[I]program2\f[R]\&...
Extract the source files embedded in the given compiled programs, each
into a \f[B]<program>\-source/\f[R] directory.
The extracted directory is a working project: it includes the
program\[cq]s sources, \f[B]packages.ini\f[R], license texts, and a
pre\-seeded package store, so it can be rebuilt as\-is without network
access.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-vendor\f[R] \f[I]package1\f[R] \f[I]package2\f[R]\&...
Copy the named packages\[cq] digest\-verified source archives into the
current project\[cq]s \f[B]vendor/\f[R] directory and update
\f[B]./packages.ini\f[R] to use the vendored copies as the primary
sources (keeping the digest pins and demoting the previous sources to
fallbacks).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-vendor\-editable\f[R] \f[I]package1\f[R] \f[I]package2\f[R]\&...
Like \f[B]\-\-vendor\f[R], but extract each package\[cq]s sources into
\f[B]vendor/<name>/\f[R] and drop its digest pin, so the vendored copy
can be edited freely.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-install\-target\f[R]
When using \f[B]\-\-target\f[R], download and install the target
platform\[cq]s libraries without asking for confirmation.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-show\-codegen\f[R] \f[I]\f[R], \f[B]\-C\f[R] \f[I]\f[R]
Set a program (e.g.\ \f[B]cat\f[R] or \f[B]bat\f[R]) to display the
generated code
.TP
\f[B]\-\-force\-rebuild\f[R], \f[B]\-f\f[R]
Force rebuilding/recompiling.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-format\f[R], \f[B]\-F\f[R] \f[I]file1.tm\f[R] \f[I]file2.tm\f[R]\&...
Autoformat the given files and print them to standard output.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-format\-inplace\f[R] \f[I]file1.tm\f[R] \f[I]file2.tm\f[R]\&...
Autoformat the given files in\-place and overwrite the original files.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-optimization\f[R] \f[B]level\f[R], \f[B]\-O\f[R] \f[B]level\f[R]
Set the optimization level.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-prefix\f[R]
Print the Tomo installation prefix and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-quiet\f[R], \f[B]\-q\f[R]
Run in quiet mode.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-source\-mapping=\f[R], \f[B]\-m=\f[R] \f[B]<yes|no>\f[R]
Toggle whether source mapping should be enabled or disabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-target\f[R] \f[I]platform\f[R]
Cross\-compile for another platform instead of this machine\[cq]s.
The target platform\[cq]s libraries are installed on demand from its
Tomo distribution archive.
Cross\-compiled executables get the platform as a filename suffix
(e.g.\ \f[B]foo.aarch64\-macos\f[R]) and cannot be run or installed on
this machine.
Valid platforms are: \f[B]x86_64\-linux\f[R], \f[B]aarch64\-linux\f[R],
\f[B]riscv64\-linux\f[R], \f[B]powerpc64le\-linux\f[R],
\f[B]s390x\-linux\f[R], \f[B]x86_64\-macos\f[R],
\f[B]aarch64\-macos\f[R], \f[B]x86_64\-freebsd\f[R],
\f[B]aarch64\-freebsd\f[R], \f[B]x86_64\-netbsd\f[R],
\f[B]aarch64\-netbsd\f[R], \f[B]x86_64\-openbsd\f[R], and
\f[B]aarch64\-openbsd\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-uninstall\f[R], \f[B]\-u\f[R] \f[I]pkg1\f[R] \f[I]pkg2\f[R]\&...
Uninstall the given packages.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R], \f[B]\-v\f[R]
Print extra verbose output.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Print the compiler version and exit.
